Author the Hub artwork at the sizes Fabric actually renders

Every image in Product.json pointed at the same square logo. The Hub
renders each field into a fixed slot and stretches whatever it is given,
so the banner behind the title was a square scaled to 1920x240 and then
clipped into an arc, the Get started card was an oversized crop, and the
At a glance carousel was one giant square.

Only the banner is validated, and it is validated strictly: exactly
1920x240, checked at upload rather than at packaging. The remaining
sizes come from the assets Microsoft's own extensibility toolkit ships,
read out of the PNG headers rather than from prose: 16:9 slides, a
320x180 Get started card, a square icon of at least 240px.

The banner is generated rather than drawn by hand so it can be
regenerated when the brand moves. Its left third stays dark because the
portal draws the workload title there in white and crops that edge at
narrow viewports; the circuitry and blooms sit centre-right, and nothing
legible goes in the file.

At a glance now carries four captures of the running workload: sync
links with lineage, the notebooks tab with its scheduler, and monitoring
in both themes - the light one included deliberately, because respecting
the host theme is a compliance point and a screenshot proves it.

Screenshots are padded to 16:9, not cropped. The captures are about
1.65:1 and cropping would cut away the interface the slide exists to
show; padding uses each capture's own edge colour so it is invisible in
either theme.

pack.ps1 copied only logo.png into the package, so any asset added here
would have been referenced and then absent. It now copies the whole
assets folder.

